WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.572
Production Razor Pages applications need
error handling that helps users and

2
00:00:07.652 --> 00:00:11.295
operators without exposing internals.

3
00:00:11.375 --> 00:00:17.375
Stack traces, connection strings, secret
names, internal paths,

4
00:00:17.455 --> 00:00:24.955
raw exception details, and sensitive data
should not appear in user-facing pages.

5
00:00:25.035 --> 00:00:30.821
Development diagnostics belong in
development environments,

6
00:00:30.901 --> 00:00:33.473
not in production behavior.

7
00:00:33.553 --> 00:00:40.839
Logs should capture useful security and
reliability signals without becoming a

8
00:00:40.919 --> 00:00:44.347
second repository of sensitive data.

9
00:00:44.427 --> 00:00:51.070
Avoid recording passwords, tokens,
secrets, raw sensitive form values,

10
00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:56.293
complete uploaded content, or excessive
personal data.

11
00:00:56.373 --> 00:01:04.088
Logs should support operations and review
while respecting data classification and

12
00:01:04.168 --> 00:01:06.525
retention expectations.

13
00:01:06.605 --> 00:01:13.355
Useful events include authentication
failures, authorization denials,

14
00:01:13.435 --> 00:01:19.971
antiforgery failures, validation failures,
unusual upload behavior,

15
00:01:20.051 --> 00:01:27.230
unexpected exceptions, administrative
changes, and configuration changes.

16
00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:32.560
The value is context: which page, which
handler category,

17
00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:39.712
which authenticated identity or
correlation identifier where appropriate,

18
00:01:39.792 --> 00:01:43.435
and what decision the application made.

19
00:01:43.515 --> 00:01:49.515
Secrets should stay outside source control
and be managed through

20
00:01:49.595 --> 00:01:53.238
environment-appropriate mechanisms.

21
00:01:53.318 --> 00:02:00.604
Development secrets, production secrets,
certificates, connection strings,

22
00:02:00.684 --> 00:02:07.006
API keys, signing material, and service
credentials need ownership,

23
00:02:07.086 --> 00:02:12.657
access control, rotation expectations, and
logging review.

24
00:02:12.737 --> 00:02:20.023
A Razor Pages application should not
depend on secrets scattered through code or

25
00:02:20.103 --> 00:02:22.246
copied into templates.

26
00:02:22.326 --> 00:02:28.112
Production readiness also includes
dependency review, HTTPS,

27
00:02:28.192 --> 00:02:34.407
security headers, backup and restore
awareness, incident handoff,

28
00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:39.308
configuration review, and ownership of
exceptions.

29
00:02:39.388 --> 00:02:46.567
A page can be secure in local testing and
still be unsafe if production settings,

30
00:02:46.647 --> 00:02:53.826
hosting behavior, logging sinks, or
deployment permissions are not reviewed.

31
00:02:53.906 --> 00:02:57.978
The practical readiness question is
simple:

32
00:02:58.058 --> 00:03:03.736
can the team explain how the application
authenticates users,

33
00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:10.888
authorizes pages, validates state changes,
handles errors, stores secrets,

34
00:03:10.968 --> 00:03:16.540
logs important decisions, and changes
safely after release.

35
00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:20.584
If those answers are documented and
tested,

36
00:03:20.664 --> 00:03:25.700
the application is much easier to operate
responsibly.
